### 1. Data and Digital Interoperability Across Care Boundaries
Across all priorities (specifically P2, P3, P5, and P8), the primary barrier to efficient care is information fragmentation. Systemic performance improves when emergency clinicians can view primary care records, advance care preferences, and social care plans in real-time. Shared electronic care records lower diagnostic spending, prevent non-beneficial resuscitative interventions, and enable safe discharges.

### 2. Multi-Disciplinary Team (MDT) Re-allocation Away from Inpatient Wards to Front-Door & Community Hubs
Transitioning key clinical skill sets—geriatricians, occupational therapists, physiotherapists, social workers, and liaison psychiatrists—directly to the front door of EDs (P1, P6) and into 24/7 community response teams (P3, P7, P10) is a critical success factor. Treating frailty and social vulnerability early prevents acute admission and hospital-acquired deconditioning.

### 3. Integrated Urgent Triage and Direct Care Navigation
Bypassing traditional ED registration via direct clinical-to-clinical booking routes (P1, P6, P7) protects acute emergency capacity. When ambulance services, NHS 111, and GPs can book directly into SDEC, Urgent Treatment Centres, Community Virtual Wards, and Mental Health Crisis Hubs, patients receive appropriate care faster without overwhelming emergency departments.

